Deputy Minister Phung Duc Tien remarked that the nation might still face its 16th storm of the year as 2025 draws to a close. Hoang Duc Cuong, Deputy Director of the Hydrometeorological Department, provided an overview of the year's natural disaster and hydro-meteorological developments, highlighting that 2025 has experienced exceptionally atypical weather patterns and disasters, resulting in significant damage across the country.

The World Meteorological Organization (WMO) is evaluating the extraordinary rainfall recorded at the Bach Ma station in Thua Thien Hue, which saw 1,739 mm of rain in a single day in October, for potential acknowledgment as one of the world’s highest recorded 24-hour rainfall totals.

The East Sea experienced an unprecedented surge in storm and tropical depression occurrences, marking the highest activity level since Vietnam began record-keeping in 1961. By the end of November, 21 storms and tropical depressions had been documented, surpassing the previous record of 20 set in 2017. Notably, Storm No. 9 (RAGASA) reached level 17 wind speeds, with gusts exceeding this level, making it the most powerful storm ever recorded in the East Sea.

The floods of 2025 were notably extensive and intense, with river levels reaching unprecedented heights across numerous areas. These floods affected regions from the Northern and Central territories to the Central Highlands and the lower Mekong Delta. For the first time in a single year, 20 rivers recorded either extreme or historic flooding. The historic flood in eastern Dak Lak, formerly part of Phu Yen province, surpassed the 1993 flood peak levels on the Ba River basin.

The pattern of intensifying weather extremes is becoming more evident, with significant variations in storms, precipitation, and flooding, indicating a shift in conventional flood patterns and highlighting the impact of climate change.
