Sri Lanka declared a state of emergency on Saturday, appealing for international aid as Cyclone Ditwah's torrential rains and floods resulted in 132 fatalities and left 176 individuals unaccounted for. According to the Disaster Management Centre (DMC), as reported by AFP, the severe weather system demolished over 15,000 residences and compelled 78,000 individuals to seek refuge in government-run temporary shelters. DMC Director-General Sampath Kotuwegoda confirmed the death toll and missing persons, noting that relief efforts have been bolstered with forces from the army, navy, and air force.

Despite the cyclone's northward trajectory toward India, new landslides occurred in Kandy, inundating several sections of the primary access road. Concurrently, worsening floods in low-lying regions led authorities to mandate evacuations for residents along the Kelani River in Colombo. The government has urged international aid and called on Sri Lankans abroad to donate funds to support nearly half a million people affected by the calamity.

India promptly responded to the crisis, dispatching two aircraft loaded with relief items, and an Indian warship docked in Colombo contributed its provisions to support victims. Prime Minister Narendra Modi expressed his condolences to the people of Sri Lanka in a post on X, lamenting the lives lost to the cyclone and reaffirming India's commitment to aiding its "closest maritime neighbor." He emphasized that India had swiftly provided relief supplies and essential Humanitarian Assistance and Disaster Relief (HADR) equipment as part of their efforts.
