data.fused_content:
The Labor government has announced a $200 million allocation to fund 96 projects nationwide, aimed at enhancing community resilience against extreme weather conditions. This initiative is part of the Disaster Ready Fund (DRF), a governmental program dedicated to improving disaster resilience and mitigating risks, with a total commitment of up to $1 billion over five years.

A significant portion of the $200 million allocation, exceeding 50%, is earmarked for infrastructure projects. These projects include the construction of cyclone shelters and evacuation centers, such as the one planned for Palm Island off the coast of Queensland, which will accommodate 800 people. Other initiatives involve establishing recovery centers, flood protection infrastructure, and sophisticated alert systems to reduce the dangers posed by bushfires.

Additional projects include a $1.6 million infrastructure undertaking aimed at safeguarding Townsvale Estate in South Australia, a $14.1 million investment for enhancing stormwater infrastructure in Narrandera, Central NSW, and $895,000 to enhance the capacity of Karajarri Rangers in northwest WA to reduce severe wildfire threats.

The DRF has previously distributed approximately $400 million in federal funding to over 350 projects, with nearly half of these projects currently in pre-construction or construction-delivery stages. According to the department, 43 projects are either approaching completion or have been fully finished.

Emergency Management Minister Kristy McBain emphasized the importance of these initiatives, stating that Australians are well-acquainted with the annual onslaught of floods, bushfires, and cyclones. The third round of funding will benefit all 538 local government areas across states and territories, elevating the total investment through the DRF to $600 million.
