Global financial losses due to natural disasters decreased significantly to $166 billion in 2025, marking a nearly 40% reduction compared to the previous year. Despite this decline, Munich Re, a German reinsurance firm, cautioned about the persistent "alarming" trend in extreme weather occurrences, with climate change likely playing a significant role.

Munich Re's annual disaster report highlighted the Los Angeles wildfires in January as the year's most expensive catastrophe, incurring total losses of $53 billion and insured damages close to $40 billion. The report also noted that the world narrowly avoided facing even greater losses by mere happenstance.

The insured losses for 2025 amounted to $108 billion, a significant decrease from the previous year. Approximately 17,200 individuals perished due to natural disasters globally, a notable increase from the roughly 11,000 recorded in 2024.

Tobias Grimm, chief climate scientist at Munich Re, described 2025 as a year possessing "two faces," with the initial six months marking unprecedented financial losses for the insurance sector, yet the latter half recorded the minimal losses of the past ten years.

Following the LA wildfires, the most expensive catastrophe of the year was a catastrophic earthquake in Myanmar, causing estimated damages of $12 billion. Hurricane Melissa, among the most powerful storms recorded to hit land, struck Jamaica, resulting in approximately $9.8 billion in damages.

Regionally, the United States faced collective losses totaling $118 billion, with $88 billion covered by insurance. The Asia-Pacific region experienced losses amounting to approximately $73 billion, yet just $9 billion of those losses were covered by insurance. Europe incurred losses amounting to $11 billion, while Africa faced $3 billion in damages from natural disasters, with insurance covering less than 20% of the losses.

Australia experienced its second-highest financial toll from natural disasters since 1980, primarily driven by a succession of intense storms and widespread flooding. The report emerges amid rising doubt about green policies, notably following the reinstatement of US President Donald Trump, who dismisses climate science as a "hoax." Munich Re emphasized that climate change is already playing a role in intensifying extreme weather, resulting in heightened humidity, intensified rainfall, and higher wind speeds.
