COLOMBO: On Sunday, Sri Lankan officials were grappling with escalating floodwaters in sections of the capital, Colombo, following the devastation caused by Cyclone Ditwah. The cyclone, which departed from Sri Lanka on Saturday, resulted in at least 159 fatalities nationwide and left 203 individuals unaccounted for, according to the Disaster Management Centre (DMC).

The Kelani River's water level continues to rise, causing flooding in the low-lying regions adjacent to its banks. Despite the cyclone's departure, intense rainfall upstream is exacerbating the situation. Over 20,000 homes have been destroyed, and approximately 833,000 people have been displaced, with around 200,000 individuals rescued and relocated to shelters.

The severe weather has led to significant disruptions, with one-third of the nation still without electricity and access to running water due to downed power lines and flooded water treatment plants. Internet access has also faced interruptions. Numerous roads in the hardest-hit central province remain impassable, complicating relief efforts.

In response to the crisis, President Anura Kumara Dissanayake declared a state of emergency on Saturday. The Sri Lankan government has sought assistance from the international community. India has dispatched relief supplies, including two helicopters and their crews for rescue operations. Japan has announced plans to send a team to assess urgent needs and has committed to providing additional support.

Military personnel from the army, navy, and air force have been deployed to assist with relief operations, working alongside civilian workers and volunteers. This cyclone is the most lethal natural disaster to hit Sri Lanka since 2017, when floods and landslides claimed over 200 lives and displaced hundreds of thousands. The most severe flooding since the beginning of the 21st century occurred in June 2003, resulting in 254 fatalities.
