Authorities in Vietnam have reported that the death toll from a week of flooding and unprecedented rainfall in central regions has risen to 40. The relentless downpours have transformed streets into waterways, overflowed riverbanks, and inundated several of the nation's historic landmarks, according to the environment ministry’s disaster management agency. The severe weather conditions are expected to persist, with Typhoon Kalmaegi projected to make landfall in the early hours of Friday. The national weather bureau forecasts that the typhoon may strike Vietnam’s coast with winds reaching up to 166 kilometers per hour (100 miles per hour) as it arrives on Thursday.

Vietnam typically experiences heavy rainfall from June to September, with an average of ten typhoons or tropical storms impacting the country each year. However, Typhoon Kalmaegi is anticipated to be the 13th storm of 2025. Scientific studies have indicated that human-induced climate change is exacerbating the frequency and severity of these weather events. Currently, the storm is affecting the Philippines, where it has caused at least two fatalities and displaced hundreds of thousands, as reported by AFP.
