The Syrian National Earthquake Center participated in an advanced workshop held in Algeria on November 11-12. Organized by the Arab Center for Earthquake and Natural Disaster Prevention, the event focused on evaluating earthquake hazards and instructing attendees on sophisticated systems.

The workshop, in partnership with the Global Earthquake Model (GEM) Foundation, brought together specialists from ten Arab nations. Participants received training on the OpenQuake Engine software, learning to craft detailed seismic hazard models and generate risk maps to assess potential earthquake effects.

Jamal Zakkour, head of the National Earthquake Center, noted that the workshop provided significant insights into Algeria's approach to earthquake risk assessment. He emphasized that the training facilitated the sharing of expertise and contemporary methods among the involved nations, enhancing the understanding of seismic phenomena and strengthening disaster response plans.

Syria's involvement aligns with its initiatives to bolster scientific readiness as part of the Ministry of Emergency and Disaster Management's strategy. Since its establishment in 2004, the Arab Center for Earthquake and Natural Disaster Prevention has aimed to enhance regional collaboration in earthquake science and the mitigation of natural disaster risks.
