A massive illegal dumping incident, believed to be the largest instance of fly-tipping in the UK, has been discovered near Kidlington in Oxfordshire. The waste, spanning 500 feet and reaching a depth of 20 feet, was discarded all at once in a field adjacent to the A34 and the River Cherwell. This illegal act has been described by an MP as "threatening an environmental disaster."

The waste pile, consisting of materials such as plastic, foam, and wood, was discovered last month by horrified anglers. Environmental groups and local representatives have expressed grave concerns about the potential ecological impact, warning of toxic run-off seeping into the river system, which could endanger wildlife and compromise the health of the entire catchment area.

The Environment Agency has launched an investigation into the incident. Specialist officers are working to identify those responsible for the illegal dumping and to enforce appropriate measures. The agency has acknowledged the challenges posed by limited resources, noting that the projected cost of removing the waste exceeds the annual budget of the local district council.

Calum Miller, representing Bicester and Woodstock for the Liberal Democrats, highlighted the issue in Parliament, emphasizing the urgency of addressing the situation. The Environment Agency has urged anyone with information about this incident or other suspected waste crimes to contact their 24-hour incident hotline.
