Typhoon Kalmaegi, locally known as Typhoon Tino, has caused significant destruction in the central Visayas region this week. The storm is the latest in a series of late-season cyclones to impact the Philippines in recent years, continuing a troubling trend of powerful tropical cyclones that have affected the nation over the last 15 years, particularly between October and December.

Kalmaegi entered Philippine territory on Sunday as a tropical storm and rapidly intensified. The fierce winds ripped roofs off houses, uprooted trees and power lines, and carried away vehicles and temporary shelters. Analysts have attributed the increasing frequency and severity of these weather events to climate change, accelerated urbanization, and inadequate disaster preparedness.

Ven Paolo Valenzuela, a research fellow at the College of Integrative Studies at Singapore Management University, highlighted the persistent lack of sufficient disaster preparedness measures, which has left the nation vulnerable to such extreme weather conditions.
