Throughout 2025, global temperatures remained at unusually high levels, highlighting the significant impact of fossil fuel-induced climate change in exacerbating extreme weather conditions. Despite La Niña conditions, which typically bring cooler global temperatures, 2025 ranked among the three hottest years on record. Since the Paris Agreement came into effect, global temperatures have climbed by approximately 0.3°C, intensifying instances of extreme heat, heavy rainfall, and conditions conducive to wildfires.

A report released on December 30, 2025, by World Weather Attribution (WWA), titled "Unequal Evidence and Impacts, Limits to Adaptation: Extreme Weather in 2025," provides a detailed examination of recent climatic phenomena. The report analyzed the ongoing combustion of coal, oil, and gas, highlighting its role in exacerbating severe weather patterns across regions and intensifying social and scientific disparities.

In 2025, WWA recorded 157 extreme weather events that fit its humanitarian impact standards. Floods and heatwaves topped the list with 49 occurrences each, followed by storms (38), wildfires (11), droughts (7), and cold spells (3). Heatwaves emerged as the most lethal occurrences of the year, with one research report suggesting that a solitary summer heatwave resulted in 24,400 deaths across Europe.

Research from 2025 revealed that human-driven climate change has exacerbated heatwaves in countries such as South Sudan, Burkina Faso, Norway, Sweden, Mexico, Argentina, and England. Studies indicated that all Atlantic hurricanes during the 2025 season intensified due to rising ocean temperatures, leading to increased destruction. Severe weather events disproportionately impact impoverished and marginalized communities, yet shortcomings in weather monitoring and climate models hinder comprehensive evaluations of numerous catastrophes in the Global South.

Numerous areas, including central Africa, western Australia, central Brazil, Canada, and extensive portions of the Middle East, faced dry spells, leading to water scarcity, agricultural setbacks, and increased risk of wildfires, marking some of their driest years on record. Research into significant fires in locations like Los Angeles and southern Spain has revealed that climate change has notably heightened the likelihood of extreme fire weather conditions.

Experts caution that while preparedness and adaptation are crucial, they alone are insufficient to match the escalating threats. The most effective strategy to circumvent the direst consequences of climate change is through swift and substantial reductions in fossil fuel consumption. Leaders must confront the fact that their persistent dependence on fossil fuels is resulting in loss of life, substantial financial setbacks, and enduring harm to communities across the globe. Without swiftly moving away from fossil fuels, extreme weather events will grow more severe, challenging societies to manage beyond their limits in the future.
