COLOMBO - Sri Lanka declared a state of emergency on Saturday as the death toll from floods and landslides caused by Cyclone Ditwah rose to 153, with 191 people reported missing. The Disaster Management Centre (DMC) reported that severe weather conditions have devastated more than 20,000 homes, forcing 108,000 individuals into government-run temporary shelters. Approximately 798,000 people require aid after being temporarily displaced.

Personnel from the army, navy, and air force, along with civilian workers and volunteers, have been mobilized to support relief operations. A passenger, recounting from the hospital, described how navy sailors used ropes to guide them safely to the roof of a nearby house above the floodwaters. A helicopter's initial rescue attempt was halted due to the rotor downdraft risking dislodging the roof, but naval boats later completed the rescue.

In the central district of Badulla, roads remain impassable, isolating several villages and blocking relief supplies. About a third of the country is without electricity or running water due to collapsed power lines and inundated water purification facilities. Saman Kumara from Maspanna, one of the hardest-hit areas in Badulla, reported that villagers are taking refuge in a temple and a single standing house, facing shortages of food and clean water.

Cyclone Ditwah departed Sri Lanka on Saturday, heading towards India. Chennai Airport in India canceled 54 flights, with forecasts predicting intense rain and high winds for the next 48 hours.

The Sri Lankan government has called for international aid and encouraged Sri Lankans abroad to contribute financially to support impacted communities. An Indian warship, visiting Colombo for a planned goodwill mission, donated its provisions to assist the victims.
