The death toll from the recent floods and landslides on Sumatra's western island has risen to 174, with approximately 80 people still missing, according to a disaster official. The National Disaster Management Agency reported that torrential monsoon rains, exacerbated by tropical cyclone Senyar in the Strait of Malacca, caused rivers in North Sumatra province to overflow on Tuesday. This led to water sweeping through mountainside villages, affecting more than 3,200 homes and structures.

In North Sumatra, fatalities have reached 116, with an additional 25 confirmed dead in Aceh. In West Sumatra, rescuers have discovered 23 bodies. Thousands of homes in parts of Aceh and West Sumatra were inundated, with many submerged up to their roofs. The disaster mitigation agency in West Sumatra noted that over 17,000 homes were flooded, forcing around 23,000 people to seek refuge in temporary shelters.

The floods and landslides have not only destroyed rice fields, livestock, and public facilities but also severed bridges and roads, isolating residents. The extreme weather, driven by tropical cyclone Senyar, triggered landslides that sent mud and rocks crashing into hilly settlements. Achadi Subarkah Raharjo from Indonesia's Meteorology, Climatology, and Geophysical Agency explained that the extreme weather warning has been extended due to a robust water vapor supply and changing atmospheric conditions. The persistent downpours have made the steep, saturated terrain particularly susceptible to disasters.

In Indonesia, an archipelago with 17,000 islands, seasonal rains often lead to flooding and landslides, impacting millions living in mountainous regions or near fertile flood plains. The ongoing unstable atmospheric conditions could prolong the extreme weather while the cyclone system remains active.
