BOISE COUNTY, Idaho — A section of State Highway 21 has reopened following a "major rockslide" that occurred earlier this month, which had initially forced its closure. The Idaho Transportation Department (ITD) announced on Tuesday that the highway is now accessible from milepost 93.7 near Grandjean to milepost 105.5 at Banner Summit.

This stretch of road, known as "Avalanche Alley," frequently experiences closures during the winter season due to the threat of avalanches and snow slides. The road had resumed operations on December 12 with reduced lanes and enhanced safety measures but was closed again for additional rock scaling efforts.

The initial rockslide, which happened overnight on December 9, led to the closure of the highway between Sourdough and Banner Summit. ITD had extended the closure through the weekend of December 19 due to safety concerns from adverse weather conditions, including strong winds and rain.

