Emergency shelters have been established in Victoria and Saanich in response to frigid temperatures and fierce winds, triggering an Extreme Weather Response across the region. The initiative is managed by Our Place Society with financial support from the District of Saanich and BC Housing.

In Saanich, Broadview United Church has launched an Extreme Weather Response (EWR) shelter, offering 20 overnight beds for those without homes. These spaces will be accessible as long as severe weather conditions meet the provincial activation standards, which may include freezing temperatures, prolonged rain, or snowfall. The EWR beds are distinct from regular nightly shelter accommodations and are specifically deployed when severe weather conditions are anticipated.

Residents of Saanich can also access shelter services provided by the Victoria Native Friendship Centre, which accommodates 50 individuals. Additionally, daytime public warming facilities are available at Saanich Commonwealth Place, Gordon Head Recreation Centre, Cedar Hill Recreation and Arts Centre, and G.R. Pearkes Recreation Centre. The operational hours of these facilities may fluctuate in response to severe weather, influenced by staffing availability.

In Victoria, the municipality has added 30 more shelter mats under the Emergency Weather Response initiative. Overnight accommodations are available at 525 Johnson St, beginning at 8 p.m. on Friday and concluding at 7 a.m. on Saturday. The facility accommodates individuals of all genders and maintains a strict no-pet policy.

Environment Canada has forecasted strong winds, with speeds increasing to 30 kilometres per hour and gusts possibly reaching 70 kilometres per hour near the Juan de Fuca Strait, before easing overnight. Overnight temperatures are expected to fall to approximately 1°C, with even chillier conditions anticipated further inland.
