North Sumatra has been experiencing severe weather conditions for several consecutive days, leading to significant flooding and landslides in the region. The Tapanuli Selatan district has been particularly affected, with the calamity resulting in the evacuation of 2,851 residents and 58 individuals sustaining injuries. Tragically, at least 38 people have lost their lives this month due to landslides triggered by intense rainfall.

The local disaster agency has deployed heavy machinery to clear debris obstructing road access to the affected areas. At least three other districts within the province have also faced similar impacts from the severe weather.

According to the Meteorology, Climatology, and Geophysics Agency, the extreme weather is attributed to cyclone seeds, which are weather formations capable of developing into tropical cyclones. The annual monsoon period, typically from June to September, often brings substantial rainfall. However, climate change has altered storm patterns, affecting both the length and severity of the season. This has resulted in increased rainfall, more frequent flash floods, and intensified wind speeds, further exacerbating the situation.

The national disaster agency BNPB continues to monitor the situation closely, as the region grapples with the aftermath of these natural disasters.
