Extreme weather has relentlessly impacted North Sumatra for several consecutive days, resulting in significant devastation. In the Tapanuli Selatan district, floods and landslides have tragically claimed the lives of eight individuals, injured 58, and forced 2,851 residents to evacuate. Additionally, approximately 13 people remain unaccounted for.

The regional disaster authority has deployed heavy machinery to clear debris obstructing roadways, facilitating access to affected areas. Besides Tapanuli Selatan, at least three other districts within the province have also experienced the adverse effects of flooding and landslides.

According to the Meteorology, Climatology, and Geophysics Agency, these extreme weather conditions are linked to cyclone seeds, which have the potential to develop into tropical cyclones. The annual monsoon season, typically from June to September, often brings intense rainfall, leading to landslides, flash floods, and the spread of waterborne diseases.

Climate change has altered storm patterns, affecting both the duration and intensity of the monsoon season. This has resulted in increased rainfall, more frequent flash floods, and stronger wind gusts. Earlier this month, landslides in Central Java caused by heavy rainfall resulted in at least 38 fatalities.
