In 2017, a lightning flash extending 515 miles from Texas to Missouri has been recognized as the longest electrical bolt in the world. This record-breaking event was captured by the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration's (NOAA) GOES-16 satellite, part of the Geostationary Operational Environmental Satellite program. The satellite, launched in 2016, documented the lightning strike on October 22, 2017, which surpassed the previous record of 477 miles.

The World Meteorological Organization (WMO) classified this lightning event as a "megaflash," characterized by its extraordinary duration and distance. The WMO noted that the lightning bolt originated within a significant thunderstorm system in the Great Plains area of the United States, a recognized "storm hotspot." The stretch from eastern Texas to near Kansas City mirrors the span from Paris to Venice in Europe, a distance that would take a car eight to nine hours to cover, or at least 90 minutes by commercial flight.

Professor Randall Cerveny, featured in Weather and Climate Extremes for the WMO, highlighted that advancements in science and technology now allow for the evaluation of extreme weather conditions, including the measurement of lightning distances. He remarked on the potential for discovering even greater extremes as more high-quality lightning measurements become available.

The United States experiences approximately 25 million cloud-to-ground lightning strikes each year, according to NOAA. The agency, along with NASA, conducts ongoing monitoring and research on lightning activity to enhance weather prediction and comprehension. The financial impact of extreme weather events, including lightning, wildfires, droughts, and heatwaves, is significant. In 2024, the National Centers for Environmental Information (NCEI) documented 27 major natural disasters in the U.S., resulting in costs totaling $182.7 billion. Meteorological forecasts predict that intensified weather phenomena, rising temperatures, and shifting climate patterns will lead to stronger storms and increased financial burdens from natural disasters in the future.
