Economists from the University of Mannheim and the European Central Bank described the findings as conservative, noting that they excluded the unprecedented wildfires that ravaged southern Europe last month. Sehrish Usman, an economist affiliated with the University of Mannheim and leading the study, remarked that the study's "timely estimates" could assist policymakers in directing aid when official data is unavailable. She emphasized that the real costs of extreme weather emerge gradually, impacting lives and livelihoods through numerous pathways beyond their initial effects.

