In response to the crisis, a $28 million investment package has been introduced, co-financed by the South Australian and federal governments. This package includes a $14 million commitment from the SA government, announced by Premier Peter Malinauskas. The funding will support clean-up efforts, recovery, research, and industry assistance.

A significant portion of the package, $8.5 million, is allocated to establishing a coastal science and research network. This initiative aims to enhance the early detection and monitoring of harmful algal bloom species. Additionally, a $3 million rapid assessment will evaluate the impact of the bloom on fish stocks and fisheries.

To further bolster research capabilities, a newly established $2 million national testing lab in South Australia will eliminate the need to send samples to New Zealand for analysis. Federal Environment Minister Murray Watt highlighted the importance of this facility in improving the ability to promptly identify and track harmful algal blooms.

The algal bloom has been described as unprecedented, underscoring the urgency for climate action. The conclusion of the Senate inquiry remains uncertain, both in terms of timing and the manner in which it will address the ongoing environmental challenges.
