A residence in Camelot Village, Chapel Hill, was severely damaged by flooding from Tropical Storm Chantal. This storm was part of a series of devastating weather events that have affected North Carolina, including Hurricane Helene, which claimed 108 lives and caused $48.8 billion in damages, making it the state's most lethal storm. Within a year, Tropical Storm Chantal claimed six lives in North Carolina and inflicted over $56 million in damages in Orange County alone, prompting Governor Stein to declare a state of emergency in 13 counties.

As a native of the Gulf Coast, I have been acutely aware of the impact of natural disasters throughout my life. At the age of three, my family evacuated New Orleans in anticipation of Hurricane Katrina. When the levees broke, my father watched as water surged down our street, submerged our front lawn, and crept up to the edge of our front porch. Fortunately, the water began to recede before it could invade our home. However, many were not as fortunate—some lost their homes and others their lives. Some who evacuated never returned to New Orleans, and I worry for my family that still lives there.

Throughout my childhood and adolescence, we experienced other large storms. None were as seriously damaging as Katrina, but it was common to wake up to felled tree branches and flooded streets. In 2021, Category 4 Hurricane Ida left New Orleans without power for over three weeks during the hottest time of the year. Unfortunately, such extreme weather experiences were not limited to my time in New Orleans.

While watching these reports in shock and horror, I listened to people calling the disaster an unavoidable “act of God.” However, the increasing frequency and severity of extreme weather is not random; it is directly attributable to human-induced climate change. To prevent future tragedies, we need accurate forecasts and warnings, timely federal relief programs, more resilient infrastructure, and climate action plans. It is mystifying that the president claimed to invest in technology to protect people from extreme weather events while simultaneously cutting support for the Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA) and renewable energy. We need to be proactive, not reactive, and that means investing in climate adaptation.

As someone just starting to establish a career and life as a young professional, I fear the upcoming years during which extreme weather will become an even more powerful and disastrous force. This is reality for my lifetime, and I want to do something about it. Without concerted environmental efforts, communities will continue to suffer, and people will needlessly die. We need to make our voices heard.
