A rapidly advancing wildfire has destroyed homes in a historic California Gold Rush town known as Chinese Camp, established around 1850 by Chinese miners expelled from a neighboring encampment. The blaze, part of the TCU September Lightning Complex, has scorched over 19 square miles across Calaveras and Tuolumne counties, with no containment as of Wednesday.

The fire, referred to as the 6-5 Fire, was ignited by lightning, according to CalFire, the state's primary firefighting agency. It is among over a dozen fires that erupted across California on Tuesday. In Chinese Camp, at least five homes, including both standalone and mobile residences, were engulfed in flames. A recreational vehicle on the premises also sustained damage.

Efforts to combat the fire included residents frantically clearing large tree branches and tossing sand onto the flames to prevent the fire from spreading. Fire officials have called for additional resources, including fire engines, bulldozers, and aircraft.

During the Gold Rush, thousands of Chinese immigrants arrived in California, facing significant persecution, including an excessive Foreign Miners Tax designed to drive them out of mining. Chinese Camp was initially named Camp Washington and was renamed to acknowledge the influx of Chinese settlers. There have been no immediate reports of casualties or fatalities.
