Hurricane Beryl marked the first significant hurricane of the 2024 Atlantic season, setting a record as the earliest category 5 hurricane. As it passed approximately 150 km south of Barbados, it struck the island as a category 3 storm, resulting in large swells and waves impacting the south and west coasts. Although it caused only moderate harm to fundamental infrastructure and did not incite a humanitarian emergency, coastal assets suffered substantial damage. The total impact of the storm was valued at 193 million Barbados dollars, representing 0.15% of the nation’s GDP. Damage constituted 58% of the total impact, followed by losses at 36%, with additional costs making up 5%.

The sectors of tourism, fisheries, agriculture, and the environment collectively represented 84% of these impacts. This premature emergence and its repercussions on Barbados underscore the growing unpredictability of climate patterns, heightening the susceptibility of Caribbean small island developing states to severe weather phenomena. It is crucial to consistently enhance all stages of disaster risk management, including risk assessment, prevention, mitigation, preparedness, and response, to empower nations and communities to effectively confront the challenges posed by extreme weather and climate change.
