Nearly 17,000 residents across Ventura and Los Angeles Counties were instructed to evacuate as the rapidly advancing Canyon Fire posed a significant threat. The Canyon Fire ignited at approximately 1:25 p.m. on Thursday, August 8, near the Lake Piru reservoir in Ventura County, initially covering an area of 30 acres. By Thursday evening, the wildfire had expanded dramatically to encompass 4,856 acres, challenging firefighters attempting to control it.

Cal Fire issued an alert at 10:48 a.m. local time, warning that the Canyon Fire was advancing swiftly and posed a threat to the communities of Hasley Canyon and Hathaway Ranch, as well as endangering the 60kv powerlines. Residents were urged to evacuate immediately, with Cal Fire emphasizing that the area was legally off-limits to the public. Los Angeles County Supervisor Kathryn Barger tweeted, urging residents to heed evacuation directives seriously and not to risk lives.

The National Interagency Coordination Center reported that, as of August 8, 42,670 fires had occurred in 2025, surpassing the ten-year average of 35,620 fires. Earlier in the year, Los Angeles experienced widespread destruction due to a series of unseasonal wildfires, and in July, a brushfire on the Grand Canyon's North Rim led to the destruction of historic structures. By August, the Gifford Fire had rapidly ascended to become California's second-largest wildfire of the year.

Extreme weather events, including fires, deluges, tropical storms, and intense heatwaves, have become increasingly frequent and hazardous. A report released by the European Commission highlighted that extreme heat events, which previously occurred once every ten years, are now experienced almost three times as frequently and are 1.2˚C hotter in temperature, according to the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change.

Evacuation centers were established by the Red Cross to assist residents from Ventura County and Los Angeles County. As of Friday, containment of the Canyon Fire had reached 25%, according to KABC.
