TUOLUMNE COUNTY, Calif. (AP) — A rapidly advancing wildfire has consumed homes in a historic California Gold Rush town, originally established around 1850 by Chinese miners who had been expelled from a neighboring camp. The blaze, which ignited from a lightning strike, swiftly expanded to cover an area of 10 square miles, prompting the evacuation of the town of Chinese Camp and nearby highways on Tuesday, according to CalFire, the state's primary firefighting agency.

As of Wednesday morning, the fire remained uncontained, with at least five homes in the rural area, characterized by a mix of standalone and mobile residences, reported to be ablaze. No injuries or fatalities have been reported so far.

On Tuesday evening, a group of individuals made a desperate attempt to prevent the fire from spreading from a neighboring house by relocating large tree branches and shoveling sand onto the flames. Their efforts continued for about 30 minutes until firefighters arrived on the scene.

Fire authorities have called for additional resources, including fire engines, bulldozers, and an aircraft, to combat the blaze. This fire is part of the TCU September Lightning Complex, which has consumed over 19 square miles across various areas in Calaveras and Tuolumne counties.

Chinese Camp was established by Chinese miners who faced discrimination and hostility, including the imposition of a steep Foreign Miners Tax intended to drive them out of the mining industry. Despite these challenges, the miners formed a settlement that became known as Chinese Camp, as noted by Visit Tuolumne County.
