The Environment Agency has successfully completed 151 flood management projects across England from July 2024 to 2025, significantly enhancing flood protection for over 24,000 homes and businesses. These initiatives, which include the construction of new defenses and upgrades to existing ones, were implemented in the year following Labour's rise to power last July. The agency aims to safeguard 52,000 properties by April 2026.

Among the completed projects are the Saltfleet to Gibraltar Point Beach Management scheme in Lincolnshire, which aims to protect 2,904 properties, and the Cockett Wick seawall improvement scheme in Jaywick, safeguarding 1,526 properties. Additionally, the £75 million Canvey Island Flood Defence Scheme in Essex renewed three kilometers of tidal defenses, offering protection to over 6,000 properties for the next 50 years.

The Government has committed to increasing flood defense spending, with £2.65 billion allocated over the next two years until April 2026, marking a £250 million increase over the previous administration's allocation. Looking ahead, the Government has pledged £7.9 billion in flood defenses from 2026 to 2036, totaling more than £10.5 billion over 12 years from 2024.

Ministers emphasize that this investment will benefit over 890,000 properties, protect businesses, safeguard farmland, and support the Government's plans to build 1.5 million new homes by 2029. Caroline Douglass, executive director of floods and coastal risk management at the Environment Agency, highlighted the growing risk of flooding due to rising sea levels and more volatile rainfall patterns. She stated, "Flooding has a devastating impact on communities across the country and will continue to worsen as extreme weather is caused by our changing climate."

The Environment Agency estimates that 6.3 million properties in England are currently at risk of flooding, with this number expected to rise to around eight million by 2050. The agency remains committed to working with the Government to deliver its long-term flood investment program, ensuring that the most vulnerable communities receive the protection they need.
